From c2c7598a41a4955ec041858c1a78bba513abc63a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:27:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: video in tgcalls-lite calls through luneos-rtc-engine

Telegram calls carried by tgcalls-lite get video the way WhatsApp calls
do: org.webosports.rtcengine owns the camera, the H.264 encoder and
decoder and the shared-memory frames the Phone app shows; this side
decides when an engine session runs and moves access units between the
engine's two SOCK_SEQPACKET sockets and the call engine.

tgcalls-video.{h,cpp} hold that logic with no TDLib or luna-service2
dependency, so test/tgcalls-video-test.cpp exercises it on its own:
- AuBridge passes the peer's units to the engine without ever
  blocking libpurple's loop, holds them back until an IDR after a drop
  or a (re)connect, and reads our encoded camera on a thread of its own.
- Controller asks for capabilities, starts a session (with TX when our
  camera is wanted and the device can encode, receive-only when the user
  only watches the peer), restarts it when the camera is toggled, stops
  it when the call ends, ignores replies to sessions it already closed,
  and passes the peer's keyframe requests to the encoder at most once a
  second.

The Phone app talks to com.palm.telegram.call as it does to the
WhatsApp connector: dial takes "video", the new changeMedia method takes
incomingVideo/outgoingVideo, and the call state carries incomingVideo,
outgoingVideo, incomingVideoState, incomingVideoOrientation and the
frame/preview paths. A wish made before the engine runs is kept and
applied when it does. audiod is told the call carries video.

Our picture is sent rotated by 90 degrees (the CVO extension); that
value has not been checked against a live call yet.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
